\DOC CASES_ON_TAC

\TYPE {CASES_ON_TAC : term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Case analysis on a term of any inductive type.

\DESCRIBE
Given a term {t} of an inductive type, {CASES_ON_TAC t} splits the goal
into one goal per constructor of that type, with the arguments of the
constructor as fresh variables.

Where {t} is a variable, the equation that says which constructor it is
is substituted away everywhere, in the goal and in the assumptions both:
the variable is gone and the constructor stands in its place. That is
what {destruct} does in Rocq and what a proof about a datatype wants.

Where {t} is not a variable the substitution may not reach every
occurrence of it, so the equation is kept as an assumption as well --
Rocq's {destruct (f x) eqn:H} -- without which the proof would have no
way back to the term it split.

{INDUCT_TAC} is the naturals and {LIST_INDUCT_TAC} is lists; this takes a
term and works for every inductive type, by looking the type up with
{cases_of}.

\FAILURE
Fails if the type of {t} is not an inductive type.

\EXAMPLE
A variable: the two cases have it replaced.
{
  # g `forall l:(A)list. LENGTH l = 0 \/ 0 < LENGTH l`;;
  # e GEN_TAC;;
  # e (CASES_ON_TAC `l:(A)list`);;
  val it : goalstack = 2 subgoals (2 total)

  `LENGTH (CONS a0 a1) = 0 \/ 0 < LENGTH (CONS a0 a1)`

  `LENGTH [] = 0 \/ 0 < LENGTH []`
}
\noindent A compound term: the equation stays, because the {l} of the
right hand side is not touched.
{
  # g `forall l:(A)list. LENGTH (TL l) <= LENGTH l`;;
  # e (GEN_TAC THEN CASES_ON_TAC `TL (l:(A)list)`);;
  val it : goalstack = 2 subgoals (2 total)

    0 [`TL l = CONS a0 a1`]

  `LENGTH (CONS a0 a1) <= LENGTH l`

    0 [`TL l = []`]

  `LENGTH [] <= LENGTH l`
}

\SEEALSO
cases_of, INDUCT_ON_TAC, STRUCT_CASES_TAC.

\ENDDOC
